Maven Version Conflicts:
=========================

✅ What is a version conflict?

Two dependencies (or their transitive dependencies) require different versions of the same library.
Example:
spring-boot-starter-web needs jackson-databind:2.15
Another library needs jackson-databind:2.13


✅ What does Maven do by default?
Maven uses the nearest definition rule:

It builds a dependency tree.
It picks the version that is closest to your project in that tree.
If both are at the same level, Maven picks the first one declared in your pom.xml.

So, Maven does not fail—it silently chooses one version and ignores the other.

✅ What are the risks if you ignore conflicts?

Runtime errors: If the library expects methods from a newer version but Maven picked an older one, you get NoSuchMethodError or ClassNotFoundException.
Unexpected behavior: Features might not work as expected.
Security issues: Older versions may have vulnerabilities.
Hard-to-debug problems: Everything compiles, but crashes at runtime.


✅ How to detect conflicts?
Run:
Shellmvn dependency:treeShow more lines
This shows all dependencies and their versions. Look for duplicates with different versions.

✅ How to fix conflicts?

Use <dependencyManagement> to enforce one version globally.
Use <exclusions> to remove unwanted transitive dependencies.
Use a BOM (Bill of Materials) like Spring Boot BOM to manage versions automatically.


✅ In short:
If you have version conflicts, Maven does not throw an error—it picks one version based on its rules. But this can cause runtime issues, so you must manage versions explicitly


Proxy settings (for internet access)
Credentials for private repositories (Nexus, Artifactory)
Mirror settings (use company Nexus instead of Maven Central)
Custom local repository path

